#subsubsection([Maintaining the Data Base], label-name: <sec:query-db>)

One important problem in designing logic programming languages is that
of arranging things so that as few irrelevant
#idx("query interpreter", sub: "data base")
#idx("data base", sub: "indexing")
#idx("indexing a data base")
data-base entries as
possible will be examined in checking a given pattern. For this purpose,
we will represent an assertion as a list whose head is a string that
represents the kind of information of the assertion.
We store the assertions in separate streams, one for each kind of information, in a table indexed by the kind. To fetch an assertion that may match a pattern, we return (to be tested using the matcher) all the stored assertions that have the same head (the same kind of information). Cleverer methods could also take advantage of information in the frame. We avoid building our criteria for indexing into the program instead we call on predicates and selectors that embody our criteria.
#idx("fetchassertions", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def fetch_assertions(pattern, frame):
    return get_indexed_assertions(pattern)
def get_indexed_assertions(pattern):
    return get_stream(index_key_of(pattern), "assertion-stream")
```)

The function #py("get_stream")
looks up a stream in the table and returns an empty stream if nothing is
stored there.

#snippet(```python
def get_stream(key1, key2):
    s = get(key1, key2)
    return None if is_none(s) else s
```)

Rules are stored similarly, using the head
of the rule conclusion.
A pattern
can match rules whose conclusions have the same head.
Thus, when fetching rules that might match a pattern
we fetch all rules whose conclusions have the same head
as the pattern.

#idx("fetchrules", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def fetch_rules(pattern, frame):
    return get_indexed_rules(pattern)
def get_indexed_rules(pattern):
    return get_stream(index_key_of(pattern), "rule-stream")
```)

The function #py("add_rule_or_assertion")
is used by
#py("query_driver_loop")
to add assertions and rules to the data base. Each item is stored in the
index.
#idx("addruleorassertion", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def add_rule_or_assertion(assertion):
    return add_rule(assertion) if is_rule(assertion) else add_assertion(assertion)
def add_assertion(assertion):
    store_assertion_in_index(assertion)
    return "ok"
def add_rule(rule):
    store_rule_in_index(rule)
    return "ok"
```)

To actually store an assertion or a rule, we store it in the appropriate stream.

#snippet(```python
def store_assertion_in_index(assertion):
    key = index_key_of(assertion)
    current_assertion_stream = get_stream(key, "assertion-stream")
    put(key, "assertion-stream", pair(assertion, lambda : (current_assertion_stream)))
def store_rule_in_index(rule):
    pattern = conclusion(rule)
    key = index_key_of(pattern)
    current_rule_stream = get_stream(key, "rule-stream")
    put(key, "rule-stream", pair(rule, lambda : (current_rule_stream)))
```)

The key under which a pattern
(an assertion or rule conclusion)
is stored in the table is the
string it starts with.

#snippet(```python
def index_key_of(pattern):
    return head(pattern)
```)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "data base")

#subsubsection([Stream Operations], label-name: <sec:query-streams>)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "stream operations")

The query system uses a few stream operations that were not presented
in chapter @chap:state.

The functions #py("stream_append_delayed")
and
#py("interleave_delayed")
are just like
#py("stream_append")
and
#py("interleave")
(section @sec:exploiting-streams),
except that they take a delayed argument (like the
#py("integral")
function
in section @sec:streams-and-delayed-evaluation).
This postpones looping in some cases (see
exercise @ex:q-why-not-append).
#idx("streamappenddelayed", decl: true)#idx("interleavedelayed", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def stream_append_delayed(s1, delayed_s2):
    return delayed_s2() if is_none(s1) else pair(head(s1), lambda : (stream_append_delayed(stream_tail(s1), delayed_s2)))
def interleave_delayed(s1, delayed_s2):
    return delayed_s2() if is_none(s1) else pair(head(s1), lambda : (interleave_delayed(delayed_s2(), lambda : (stream_tail(s1)))))
```)

The function #py("stream_flatmap"),
which is used throughout the query evaluator to map a
function
over a stream of frames and combine the resulting streams of frames,
is the stream analog of the #py("flatmap")
function
introduced for ordinary lists in
section @sec:nested-mappings. Unlike ordinary
#py("flatmap"), however, we accumulate the streams
with an interleaving process, rather than simply appending them (see
exercises @ex:q-why-interleave
and @ex:q-why-delay).
#idx("streamflatmap", decl: true)#idx("flattenstream", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def stream_flatmap(fun, s):
    return flatten_stream(stream_map(fun, s))
def flatten_stream(stream):
    return None if is_none(stream) else interleave_delayed(head(stream), lambda : (flatten_stream(stream_tail(stream))))
```)

The evaluator also uses the following simple
function
to generate a stream consisting of a single element:
#idx("singletonstream", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def singleton_stream(x):
    return pair(x, lambda : (None))
```)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "stream operations")

#subsubsection([Query Syntax Functions and Instantiation], label-name: <sec:query-syntax>)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "syntax of query language")
#idx("query-language-specific representation")

We saw in section @sec:query-driver that
the driver loop first transforms an input string into the Python
syntax representation.
The input is designed to look like a
Python expression so that we can use the
#py("parse") function
from section @sec:representing-expressions and
also to support Python notation in
#py("javascript_predicate").
For example,

#snippet(```python
parse('job($x, list("computer", "wizard"));')
```)

yields

#output(```python
parse('job($x, list("computer", "wizard"));')
```)

The tag
#py("\"application\"") indicates that
syntactically,
the query would be treated as a function application in JavaScipt.
The function
#py("unparse") transforms the syntax
back into a string:

#snippet(```python
unparse(parse('job($x, list("computer", "wizard"));'))
```)

#output(```python
unparse(parse('job($x, list("computer", "wizard"));'))
```)

In the query processor, we assumed a
query-language-specific
representation of assertions, rules, and queries.
The function
#py("convert_to_query_syntax")
transforms the syntax representation into that representation.
Using the same example,

#snippet(```python
convert_to_query_syntax(parse('job($x, list("computer", "wizard"));'))
```)

yields

#output(```python
convert_to_query_syntax(parse('job($x, list("computer", "wizard"));'))
```)

Query-system functions such as
#py("add_rule_or_assertion")
in section @sec:query-db
and
#py("evaluate_query")
in section @sec:query-eval
operate on the query-language-specific representation using
selectors and predicates such as
#py("type"),
#py("contents"),
#py("is_rule"), and
#py("first_conjunct") declared below.
Figure @fig:syntax-abstraction-lp depicts the three
#idx("abstraction barriers", sub: "in query language")
abstraction barriers used by the query system and how the transformation functions
#py("parse"),
#py("unparse"), and
#py("convert_to_query_syntax") bridge them.

#sicp-figure(image("/images/img_javascript/ch4-syntax-abstraction-logic-programming.svg", width: 70%), caption: [Syntax abstraction in the query system.], label-name: <fig:syntax-abstraction-lp>)

#subheading([Handling pattern variables])

The predicate
#py("is_variable") is used on the
query-language-specific representation during query processing and
on the Python syntax representation during instantiation
to identify names that start with a dollar sign.
#idx("query interpreter", sub: "pattern-variable representation")
#idx("pattern variable", sub: "representation of")
We assume there is a function #py("char_at") that
returns a string containing only the character of the given string
at the given position.#footnote[The actual way to get the string that
contains the first
character of a string #py("s") in JavaScript is
#py("s.charAt(0)").]
#idx("isvariable", sub: "in query system", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def is_variable(exp):
    return is_name(exp) and char_at(symbol_of_name(exp), 0) == "$"
```)

Unique variables are constructed during rule application
(in section @sec:query-unify) by means of the
following functions.
The unique identifier for a rule application is a number, which is
incremented each time a rule is applied.#footnote[Creating
new variables with string concatenation and identifying
variables by checking their first character during query
processing is somewhat wasteful. A more efficient solution
would mark pattern variables with a separate tag in the
query-language-specific representation and use pair
construction rather than string concatenation to create
new variables. We chose the less efficient solution
to simplify the presentation.]
#idx("makenewvariable", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
rule_counter = 0
def new_rule_application_id():
    rule_counter = rule_counter + 1
    return rule_counter
def make_new_variable(variable, rule_application_id):
    return make_name(symbol_of_name(variable) + "_" + stringify(rule_application_id))
```)

#subheading([The function #py("convert_to_query_syntax")])

The function #py("convert_to_query_syntax")
recursively
#idx("query-language-specific representation", sub: "transforming Python syntax into")
transforms the Python syntax representation into
the query-language-specific representation by simplifying
assertions, rules, and queries such that the symbol of a name in a
function expression of an application becomes a tag, except that if
the symbol is #py("\"pair\"")
or #py("\"list\""), an (untagged) Python pair
or list is built. This means that
#py("convert_to_query_syntax")
interprets
applications of
the constructors #py("pair") and
#py("list") during the transformation,
and processing functions such as
#py("pattern_match")
of section @sec:query-match and
#py("unify_match")
of section @sec:query-unify
can operate directly on the intended pairs and
lists rather than on the syntax representation generated by the parser.
The (one-element) "argument" list of
#py("javascript_predicate")
remains unprocessed, as explained below.
A variable remains unchanged, and
a literal is simplified to the primitive value it contains.
#idx("converttoquerysyntax", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def convert_to_query_syntax(exp):
    if is_application(exp):
        function_symbol = symbol_of_name(function_expression(exp))
        if function_symbol == "javascript_predicate":
            return pair(function_symbol, arg_expressions(exp))
        else:
            processed_args = map(convert_to_query_syntax, arg_expressions(exp))
            return pair(head(processed_args), head(tail(processed_args))) if function_symbol == "pair" else processed_args if function_symbol == "list" else pair(function_symbol, processed_args)
    elif is_variable(exp):
        return exp
    else:
        return literal_value(exp)
```)

An exception to this processing is
#py("javascript_predicate").
Since the instantiated Python syntax
representation
of its predicate expression is passed to
#py("evaluate")
of section @sec:core-of-evaluator,
the original syntax representation coming from
#py("parse")
needs to remain intact in the query-language-specific representation
of the expression.
In this example
of section @sec:deductive-info-retrieval

#snippet(```python
and(salary($person, $amount), javascript_predicate($amount > 50000))
```)

#py("convert_to_query_syntax") produces a data structure
in which a Python syntax representation is embedded in
a query-language-specific representation:

#output(```python
and(salary($person, $amount), javascript_predicate($amount > 50000))
```)

In order to evaluate the #py("javascript_predicate") subexpression
of that processed query, the function
#py("javascript_predicate") in
section @sec:query-eval calls the function
#py("instantiate_expression") (below)
on the embedded Python syntax
representation of #py("$amount > 50000") to
replace the variable
#py("list(\"name\", \"$amount\")") by a literal,
for example #py("list(\"literal\", 70000)"), that represents
the primitive value to which
#py("$amount") is bound, here 70000.
The Python evaluator can evaluate the instantiated predicate, which now represents
#py("70000 > 50000").

#subheading([Instantiating an expression])

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "instantiation")

The function #py("javascript_predicate")
of section @sec:query-eval and the driver loop
of section @sec:query-driver call
#py("instantiate_expression") on an
expression to obtain a copy in which any variable in the
expression is replaced by its value in a given frame.
The input and result expressions use the Python syntax
representation, so any value that results from instantiating a
variable needs to be converted from its form in the binding to
the Python syntax representation.
#idx("instantiateexpression", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def instantiate_expression(expression, frame):
    return convert(instantiate_term(expression, frame)) if is_variable(expression) else pair(instantiate_expression(head(expression), frame), instantiate_expression(tail(expression), frame)) if is_pair(expression) else expression
```)

The function
#py("instantiate_term") takes
a variable, pair, or primitive value as first argument and a frame
as second argument and recursively replaces the variables in
the first argument by their values in the frame
until a primitive value or an unbound variable is reached.
When the process encounters a pair, a new pair is constructed
whose parts are the instantiated versions of the original parts.
For example, if
#py("$x")
is bound to the pair
$[mono("$y"), 5]$
in a frame $f$
as the result of unification, and
#py("$y")
is in turn bound to 3,
the result of applying
#py("instantiate_term") to
#py("list(\"name\", \"$x\")") and
$f$ is the pair
$[3, 5]$.
#idx("instantiateterm", decl: true)
#syntax("
def instantiate_term(term, frame):
    if is_variable(term):
        binding = binding_in_frame(term, frame)
        return term if is_none(binding) else instantiate_term(binding_value(binding), frame)
    elif is_pair(term):
        return pair(instantiate_term(head(term), frame), instantiate_term(tail(term), frame))
    else:
        return term
")

The function
#py("convert")
constructs a Python syntax representation for a variable,
pair, or primitive value returned by
#py("instantiate_term").
A pair in the original becomes an application of Python's
pair constructor and a primitive value becomes a literal.
#idx("convert", decl: true)
#syntax("
def convert(term):
    return term if is_variable(term) else make_application(make_name(\"pair\"), llist(convert(head(term)), convert(tail(term)))) if is_pair(term) else make_literal(term)
")

To illustrate these three functions, consider what happens when the query

#snippet(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

whose Python syntax representation is given
at the beginning of section @sec:query-syntax, is
processed by the driver loop.
Let's say a frame $g$ of the result stream
binds the variable
#py("$x") to the pair
$[mono("\"Bitdiddle\""), mono("$y")]$ and
the variable #py("$y") to the pair
$[mono("\"Ben\""), mono("null")]$. Then

#syntax("
instantiate_term(llist(\"name\", \"$\\$$x\"), ", meta("g"), ")
	    ")

returns the list

#output(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

which
#py("convert") transforms into

#output(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

The result of
#py("instantiate_expression")
applied to the Python syntax representation of the query and
the frame $g$ is:

#output(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

The driver loop unparses this representation and displays it as:

#output(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "instantiation")

#subheading([The function #py("unparse")])

The function #py("unparse")
transforms a component given in the Python syntax representation
into a string by applying the syntax rules
of section @sec:representing-expressions.
We describe #py("unparse") only for
those kinds of expressions that appear in the examples of
section @sec:deductive-info-retrieval, leaving statements
and the remaining kinds of expressions
as exercise @ex:parse. A literal is transformed by
#py("stringify")ing its value, and
a name is transformed into its
#idx("symbol(s)", sub: "in unparse")
symbol.
An application is formatted by unparsing the function expression,
which we can assume to be a name here, followed by the comma-separated
argument expression strings enclosed in parentheses.
Binary operator combinations are formatted using infix notation.
#idx("unparse", sub: "in query interpreter", decl: true)
#syntax("
function unparse(exp) {
    return is_literal(exp)
           ? stringify(literal_value(exp))
           : is_name(exp)
           ? symbol_of_name(exp)
           : is_list_construction(exp)
           ? unparse(make_application(make_name(\"list\"),
                                      element_expressions(exp)))
           : is_application(exp) && is_name(function_expression(exp))
           ? symbol_of_name(function_expression(exp)) +
                 \"(\" +
                 comma_separated(map(unparse, arg_expressions(exp))) +
                 \")\"
           : is_binary_operator_combination(exp)
           ? \"(\" + unparse(first_operand(exp)) +
             \" \" + operator_symbol(exp) +
             \" \" + unparse(second_operand(exp)) +
             \")\"
           ", metaphrase[unparsing other kinds of Python components], "
           : error(exp, \"unknown syntax -- unparse\")
}
	    ")

#snippet(```python
def comma_separated(strings):
    return reduce(lambda s, acc: (s + ("" if acc == "" else ", " + acc)), "", strings)
```)

The function
#py("unparse")
would work fine without the clause

#snippet(```python
: is_list_construction(exp)
? unparse(make_application(make_name("list"),
                           element_expressions(exp)))
```)

but the output string would be unnecessarily
verbose in cases where pattern variables are instantiated
by lists. In the example above, where processing the query

#snippet(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

yields a frame that binds
#py("$x")
to $[mono("\"Bitdiddle\""), [mono("\"Ben\""), mono("null")]]$,
#py("unparse") produces

#output(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

However, without the clause it would produce

#output(```python
job($x, llist("computer", "wizard"))
```)

which explicitly constructs the two pairs that make up the first list.
To achieve the more concise formatting used throughout
section @sec:deductive-info-retrieval,
we inserted the clause to check
if the expression constructs a list, in which case
we format it as a single application of
#py("list") to the list of element
expressions that we extract from the expression.
A list construction is the literal
#py("null") or
an application of
#py("pair")
whose second argument is itself a list construction.
#idx("islistconstruction", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def is_list_construction(exp):
    return (is_literal(exp) and is_none(literal_value(exp))) or (is_application(exp) and is_name(function_expression(exp)) and symbol_of_name(function_expression(exp)) == "pair" and is_list_construction(head(tail(arg_expressions(exp)))))
```)

Extracting the element expressions from a given list construction
amounts to collecting the first arguments of applications of
#py("pair") until the literal
#py("null") is reached.
#idx("elementexpressions", decl: true)
#syntax("
def element_expressions(list_constr):
    return None if is_literal(list_constr) else pair(head(arg_expressions(list_constr)), element_expressions(head(tail(arg_expressions(list_constr)))))
")

#subheading([Predicates and selectors for the query-language-specific representation])

The functions #py("type")
and #py("contents"), used by
#py("evaluate_query")
(section @sec:query-eval), specify that a
syntactic form of a query-language-specific representation
is identified by
the string in its head.
They are the same as the
#py("type_tag")
and #py("contents")
functions
in section @sec:manifest-types, except for the
error message.
#idx("type in query system", decl: true)#idx("contents", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def type(exp):
    return head(exp) if is_pair(exp) else error("unknown expression type", exp)
def contents(exp):
    return tail(exp) if is_pair(exp) else error("unknown expression contents", exp)
```)

The following functions, used by
#py("query_driver_loop")
(in section @sec:query-driver),
specify that rules and assertions are added to the data base by an
#py("assert") command, which the function
#py("convert_to_query_syntax") transforms
into a pair of the form
#py("[\"assert\",")#meta("rule-or-assertion")#py("]"):
#idx("isassertion", decl: true)#idx("assertionbody", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def is_assertion(exp):
    return type(exp) == "assert"
def assertion_body(exp):
    return head(contents(exp))
```)

Here are the declarations of the predicates and selectors
for the #py("and"),
#py("or"), #py("not"), and
#py("javascript_predicate")
syntactic forms
(section @sec:query-eval):
#idx("isemptyconjunction", decl: true)#idx("firstconjunct", decl: true)#idx("restconjuncts", decl: true)#idx("isemptydisjunction", decl: true)#idx("firstdisjunct", decl: true)#idx("restdisjuncts", decl: true)#idx("negatedquery", decl: true)#idx("javascriptpredicateexpression", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def is_empty_conjunction(exps):
    return is_none(exps)

def first_conjunct(exps):
    return head(exps)

def rest_conjuncts(exps):
    return tail(exps)
def is_empty_disjunction(exps):
    return is_none(exps)

def first_disjunct(exps):
    return head(exps)

def rest_disjuncts(exps):
    return tail(exps)
def negated_query(exps):
    return head(exps)
def javascript_predicate_expression(exps):
    return head(exps)
```)

The following three
functions
define the query-language-specific representation of rules:
#idx("isrule", decl: true)#idx("conclusion", decl: true)#idx("rulebody", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def is_rule(assertion):
    return is_tagged_list(assertion, "rule")
def conclusion(rule):
    return head(tail(rule))

def rule_body(rule):
    return llist("always_true") if is_none(tail(tail(rule))) else head(tail(tail(rule)))
```)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "syntax of query language")
#idx("query-language-specific representation")

#subsubsection([Frames and Bindings], label-name: <sec:query-bindings>)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "frame")
#idx("frame (query interpreter)", sub: "representation")

Frames are represented as lists of bindings, which are
variable-value pairs:
#idx("makebinding", decl: true)#idx("bindingvariable", decl: true)#idx("bindingvalue", decl: true)#idx("bindinginframe", decl: true)#idx("extend", decl: true)
#snippet(```python
def make_binding(variable, value):
    return pair(variable, value)
def binding_variable(binding):
    return head(binding)
def binding_value(binding):
    return tail(binding)
def binding_in_frame(variable, frame):
    return assoc(variable, frame)
def extend(variable, value, frame):
    return pair(make_binding(variable, value), frame)
```)

#idx("query interpreter", sub: "frame")

